"Does any word start with `ca`?" is a search that skips the `isWord` check: if the path for the prefix exists, some word continues it. One helper serves both questions. `find` walks a prefix and returns the node it ends on, or nil:

```go harness
type TrieNode struct {
	children [26]*TrieNode
	isWord   bool
	count    int // how many words pass through this node
}

func (t *TrieNode) Insert(word string) {
	node := t
	for i := 0; i < len(word); i++ {
		c := word[i] - 'a'
		if node.children[c] == nil {
			node.children[c] = &TrieNode{}
		}
		node = node.children[c]
		node.count++ // one more word goes through here
	}
	node.isWord = true
}

{{code}}

trie := &TrieNode{}
trie.Insert("cat")
fmt.Println(trie.find("ca") != nil, trie.find("co") != nil)
```

```go
// find follows prefix down from t, or returns nil when the path breaks off.
func (t *TrieNode) find(prefix string) *TrieNode {
	node := t
	for i := 0; i < len(prefix) && node != nil; i++ {
		node = node.children[prefix[i]-'a']
	}
	return node
}
```

Then a prefix exists when `find(p) != nil`, and a word exists when `find(w) != nil && find(w).isWord`.

To count the words that start with a prefix, store a `count` in every node and add 1 to each node a word passes through while you insert it. The count on the prefix's last node is the answer, with no walk through the words below it:

```go harness
{{code}}

trie := &TrieNode{}
trie.Insert("car")
trie.Insert("cat")
fmt.Println(trie.children['c'-'a'].count, trie.children['c'-'a'].children['a'-'a'].children['t'-'a'].count)
```

```go
type TrieNode struct {
	children [26]*TrieNode
	isWord   bool
	count    int // how many words pass through this node
}

func (t *TrieNode) Insert(word string) {
