steps.body:
A pattern like `c.t`, where `.` stands for any one letter, can't follow a single path. At a letter it moves to that child, and at a dot it has to try **every** child. Recursion handles the branching, one call per pattern position:

```go harness
type TrieNode struct {
	children [26]*TrieNode // one slot per letter, nil when no word continues that way
	isWord   bool          // a word ends here
}

func (t *TrieNode) Insert(word string) {
	node := t
	for i := 0; i < len(word); i++ {
		c := word[i] - 'a' // 'a' is 0, 'z' is 25
		if node.children[c] == nil {
			node.children[c] = &TrieNode{}
		}
		node = node.children[c]
	}
	node.isWord = true
}

{{code}}

trie := &TrieNode{}
for _, w := range []string{"cat", "cot", "cart"} {
	trie.Insert(w)
}
fmt.Println(match(trie, "c.t", 0), match(trie, "c.r", 0), match(trie, "c..t", 0))
```

```go
// match reports whether a word below node matches pattern from index i on.
func match(node *TrieNode, pattern string, i int) bool {
	if node == nil {
		return false
	}
	if i == len(pattern) {
		return node.isWord
	}
	if pattern[i] == '.' {
		for _, child := range node.children {
			if match(child, pattern, i+1) {
				return true
			}
		}
		return false
	}
	return match(node.children[pattern[i]-'a'], pattern, i+1)
}
```

- A nil node is a dead end, which also covers the nil children a dot tries. Checking it first keeps the rest of the function simple.
- At the end of the pattern, only a node marked `isWord` counts as a match. `c.r` reaches the r node of `cart`, which isn't a word end, so it's false.
- `return true` as soon as one child matches. There's no reason to look at the others.

Without dots this is a plain search, one step per letter. Each dot can multiply the work by up to 26, but the trie only branches where words really continue, so it stays far cheaper than checking every word.

